<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=stainless-steel">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stainless steel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Inox, Rustless steel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stainless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> is an iron-based alloy containing chromium of 10.5% or more, which forms a passive film that resists rust and corrosion. Chromium-iron alloys were observed to resist oxidising agents in the early 1800s, and between 1904 and 1911 researchers including Leon Guillet prepared alloys later considered stainless. On 17 October 1912 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a> engineers Benno Strauss and Eduard Maurer patented the austenitic steel Nirosta, later known as 18/8 or type 304. In 1913 Harry Brearley in Sheffield discovered a martensitic alloy, announced in January 1915; he and a cutlery maker named it stainless steel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stainless-steel.jpg" alt="Stainless steel" width="330" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W.carter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sink_and_taps_in_the_men's_locker_room_3_BW.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Freedom Sculpture, a <b>stainless steel</b> work by Cecil Balmond in Century City, California, is modelled on it.</blockquote><a href="/work/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The restoration of 1984 to 1986 replaced Eiffel's entire puddled iron armature with <b>stainless steel</b> bars of Ferralium, and replaced the torch, now covered in 24-karat gold leaf, with a replica of Bartholdi's original.</blockquote><a href="/work/statue-of-liberty">Statue of Liberty · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Original mortar, plaster and decoration were strengthened and preserved in place, cement additions were removed, corroded iron clamps were replaced with <b>stainless steel</b>, and cracks in the main dome were consolidated by injection-based reinforcement.</blockquote><a href="/work/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1912</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Essen, Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Benno Strauss · Eduard Maurer · Harry Brearley · <a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Sheffield, England (1913); United States (1912)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Harry Brearley is usually credited with discovery in 1913; Elwood Haynes applied for a US patent on a martensitic alloy in 1912 that was not granted until 1919. The corrosion resistance of iron-chromium alloys may have been first recognised in 1821 by Pierre Berthier.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/bessemer-process">Bessemer process<span>1856</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=stainless-steel">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
